  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heinz bodies occur in red blood cells (RBCs) following damage from chemical or dietary",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "oxidants, can cause hemolytic anemia.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "When oxidants overwhelm protective reductive pathways in RBCs, denaturation of hemoglobin (Hb) causes precipitation and attachment to",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cell membrane.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "RBCs with Heinz bodies are removed by splenic macrophages, and occasionally undergo intravascular lysis,",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "forming ghost cells.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "The spleen may occasionally remove Heinz bodies, resulting in",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "spherocytes.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cats more susceptible to Heinz body formation because have less reductive enzymatic capacity and closed splenic circulation, preventing macrophages from",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "removing Heinz bodies.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Healthy cats may have low numbers of Heinz bodies without",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "anemia.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heinz bodies reported in patients with hyperthyroidism, lymphoma, and diabetes",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "mellitus.",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Heinz bodies may be accompanied by methemoglobinemia (Hb containing Fe3+) and/or eccentrocytes (indicating oxidative damage to RBC membranes).",
              "pdf_page": 165,
              "printed_page": 83,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
